Take $f_n(x) = x^n$ on $[0, 1]$. For $0 \le x < 1$ we have $x^n \to 0$, while $f_n(1) = 1$ for every $n$. So the pointwise limit is
$$f(x) = \begin{cases} 0, & 0 \le x < 1, \\ 1, & x = 1. \end{cases}$$
Every $f_n$ is a polynomial, hence continuous, but $f$ jumps at $x = 1$. Continuity has been lost in the limit.

Look at how fast the convergence happens. The least $n$ with $x^n < 0.01$ is:
- $n = 7$ for $x = 0.5$;
- $n = 44$ for $x = 0.9$;
- $n = 459$ for $x = 0.99$;
- $n = 4603$ for $x = 0.999$.

The condition $x^n < 0.01$ means $n > \ln(0.01)/\ln x$, and as $x \to 1$ the denominator $\ln x$ tends to $0$, so the required $n$ grows without bound. No single $N$ works for all $x \in [0, 1)$ at once: for every $n$, the point $x_n = 2^{-1/n}$ lies in $[0, 1)$ and $f_n(x_n) = \frac12$. In terms of graphs, $x^n$ always climbs from nearly $0$ up to $1$ in a narrow strip just left of $x = 1$; as $n$ grows the climb moves to the right but never disappears.
